**定义 76.3（finite historical replies）。** At precision $b\geq1$, a canonical plateau reply consists of its reply mode, gamma($b$), and exactly nine signed-numerator fields in historical order $0,1,2$. The expected precision is supplied with the request and must equal the represented precision. Require $|n_{ik}|\leq2^{b+1}$; set $y_i=(n_{i1},n_{i2},n_{i3})2^{-b}$. Reject trailing symbols and all invalid fields. The simultaneous semantic promise is

$$
\|y_i-x_i\|_2\leq2^{-b},\qquad\|x_i\|_2\leq1,\qquad i=0,1,2,
$$

for the same actual history at every request. No orthogonality, consistency with a descriptor, independence of errors or correct computed orientation is imposed on the replies. Source truth and the approximation promise belong to the supplier; the integer parser cannot establish them. The reply has length at most $18b+2\log_2(b+1)+80$. Outbound requests use a separate finite two-command grammar. The discovery header is the fixed eight-bit word $01011010$ with no following field; an archive session starts at $b=1$, serves that precision, and doubles its stored precision after each discovery command. The final header is the fixed eight-bit word $01011011$ followed by gamma($b_f$), with $b_f\geq1$, and requests exactly that final precision (a repeated precision is allowed). Only the final arbitrary precision is transmitted as a natural field; the discovery schedule is represented by bounded commands. The archive keeps the current finite integer $b$, and its bit width, doubling updates and setup are paid work. A request stream accepts only zero or more discovery commands followed by one final command, and rejects unknown headers, missing or truncated fields, a zero final precision, and trailing data. An archive may receive several requests about these three stored historical values before closure; this is separately paid archive access and supplies no new destructive source Read. Fixed original reports contain only one triple and cannot be upgraded by subsequently invoking this interface. Outside the unit-read plateau, the initial-read envelope in §87 applies; the unit reply range is not asserted for every zero-regime initial history.

